{
  "capture": {
    "source": "Live reads against Dispatcharr 0.28.2 (doc-test instance) through ECM's own DispatcharrClient: GET /api/schema/?format=json, GET /api/channels/recurring-rules/, GET /api/channels/dvr/comskip-config/, GET /api/dvr/rules/ (bead enhancedchannelmanager-lsa0s)",
    "fixture_kind": "literal slice of real responses",
    "literal_raw_capture": true,
    "captured_at": "2026-08-03",
    "dispatcharr_version": "0.28.2",
    "redaction": "Nothing was edited. The doc-test instance has zero recurring rules and no custom comskip.ini, so both live bodies are operator-data-free by construction ([] and {\"path\": \"\", \"exists\": false}). The dead /api/dvr/rules/ capture keeps only the status line, content-type and the SPA <title> marker -- the rest of that body is the Dispatcharr SPA shell with build-hashed asset names, which carries no contract meaning.",
    "why": "Pins three contract facts behind the dvr_rules remap: (1) ECM's former _DVR_RULES_PATH '/api/dvr/rules/' has NO route on 0.28.2 -- it falls through to the SPA catch-all and returns 200 text/html, which is why get_dvr_rules() raised a JSONDecodeError and the backup exporter emitted a _warning stub for the category on every run; (2) the real DVR-rule resource is /api/channels/recurring-rules/ (RecurringRecordingRule: name identity + a single 'channel' FK + full list/create/delete CRUD), which is exactly the shape the settings_agents DVR importer already assumes; (3) /api/channels/dvr/comskip-config/ DOES exist (contradicting the old comment in routers/backup.py) but its GET returns only {path, exists} -- never the comskip.ini CONTENT -- and its POST takes a multipart .ini upload, so it is not a usable backup source."
  },
